{"title":"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Remark_and_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remark and Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/4/d0461fe8d09e9ec7b5fe6e81320800dfbaf0cb5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2, a_3, \\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"11\" /> be an infinite increasing sequence of positive integers. Prove that for every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/5/7a51ce59afdd4b396899db46e9ca0f9f3a44c321.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\geq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> there are infinitely many <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/d/1ddf57a1507686a49e3d290b3542ccac565cd35a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"10\" /> which can be written in the form<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/9/eb9bb151d31503625e3290d5c42f87bcda8b767e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_m = xa_p + ya_q\\]\" width=\"121\" height=\"15\" />with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/5/32581661cddf01ad30c1e36efe72f99eb0a0adca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" /> positive integers and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/e/01efc203b4b068c03e09fa07bd651b172aaf2a35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q &gt; p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If we can find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/3/4c3fc55368bed2cfb6f468b82dd58a9052879ee1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\ne q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"17\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/e/41eb4fea0f6fcb2f68e632441431b637ebc23ab2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_p,a_q)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"88\" height=\"18\" />, we're done: every sufficiently large positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> can be written in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/6/5b6ea7aed96f14c25274fed59cc6159390f99354.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xa_p+ya_q,\\ x,y\\in\\mathbb N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"153\" height=\"16\" />. We can thus assume there are no two such <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/3/4c3fc55368bed2cfb6f468b82dd58a9052879ee1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\ne q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"17\" />. We now prove the assertion by induction on the first term of the sequence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1ab39d761413804680d26d972381f028001562f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" />. The base step is basically proven, since if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/0/2b02a491e6fc8db3a577b344b1a53b7a8e77e0f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" /> we can take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/1/9910fc8ed8a58c9874860c7482132fb871b8c502.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> and any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/d/2cd7aa6d644eb2fff7a78bbc41e2b609a5da1987.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"41\" height=\"15\" /> we want. There must be a prime divisor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/0/b60f20eafeb607f3e152811ac941a009ed821d37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u|a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> which divides infinitely many terms of the sequence, which form some subsequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/4/c44b8eb1c0747ddbdf9e4430189a9e5b57551898.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_{k_n})_{n\\ge 1},\\ k_1=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"128\" height=\"18\" />. Now apply the induction hypothesis to the sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/d/88d2b97ad704caf4b371fb67695f8dcdaca70809.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\frac{a_{k_n}}u\\right)_{n\\ge 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"75\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>The above solution was posted and copyrighted by grobber.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remark_and_Solution_2\">Remark and Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Remark and Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This is flawed. At Least it is not clearly shown how a sequence with infinite number of terms that are coprime to a(p) is achieved by simply repeating the procedure as described.\n</p><p>Following proof would be much more convincing.\nFor every p&gt;=1 we calculate the remainder of each term of the sequence modulo a(p) and we will have a(p) types of remainders: 1~a(p). There are infinitely many terms and limited types of remainders therefore, there must be one remainder which belongs to infinitely many terms. Let it be r, 1&lt;=r&lt;=a(p), the first term of such remainder be a(q) and the subsequent terms be a(m), m towards infinity. Obviously a(m)&gt;a(q)&gt;=a(p)+1, and m&gt;q&gt;p. Pick any a(m)&gt;a(q), we now prove that it can always be written in the form a(m)=xa(p)+ya(q). Let a(q)=k1•a(p)+r, a(m)=k2•a(p)+r, k2&gt;k1. Then a(m)-a(q)=(k2-k1)a(p), a(m)=(k2-k1)a(p)+1•a(q). Let x=k2-k1, y=1, and obviously both are positive integers so we have achieved the target form. As there are infinitely many a(m), for every p&gt;=1, we will always have infinitely many a(m) which can be written in the target form. Done.\n</p><p>The above remark and solution was written by Tmath2024.\n</p><p><br />\nThe original thread for this problem can be found here: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"https://aops.com/community/p367494\">[1]</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO\" title=\"1975 IMO\">1975 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1975_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1975 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017172015\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.021 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.039 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 125/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 895/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100